  "worked_solution": "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡((x-4)^{2})-(x-6))^{′}=2(x-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((x-4)^{2})-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({x}_{n}-4)^{2})-({x}_{n}-6)}{2({x}_{n}-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({x}_{n}-4)^{2})-1} \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=5.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({x}_{0}-4)^{2})-({x}_{0}-6)}{2({x}_{0}-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({x}_{0}-4)^{2})-1} \\\\  & =5.5-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2.25)+0.5}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2.25)-1} \\\\  & ≈5.943,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
